WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.660
It's great to be back from vacation and I
will talk about what I did on the bonus show,

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:06.950
but we have to jump right into it.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:11.360
We learned at the start of this weekend that
Supreme Court Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.620
passed away at the age of 87.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:19.770
And this is simultaneously set off a nationwide.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:25.670
Really a global mourning period for one of
the premier progressive jurists.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:34.460
A truly remarkable legal mind whose legal
acumen was surpassed maybe only by her wit

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.850
and sense of humor.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:42.870
And it has set off a political firestorm,
immediately refreshing the battle from the

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:48.860
last year of Barack Obama's presidency over
the nomination of Merrick Garland, then to

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:53.300
replace the late Supreme Court Justice Antonin
Scalia.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:59.500
That was a nomination, remember, which Republicans
led by Mitch McConnell successfully blocked.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.120
And we're going to be talking about all of
it.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.420
Now, first of all, is the personal.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.579
I'm going to focus on the political.

00:01:06.579 --> 00:01:10.520
But Ruth Bader Ginsburg's personal story is
an incredible one.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:17.639
Born in Brooklyn, at a time when the legal
world was a big uphill battle not just for

00:01:17.639 --> 00:01:19.749
women, but for Jewish women.

00:01:19.749 --> 00:01:27.869
A tiny woman barely five feet tall, ultimately
becoming the second woman ever to be on the

00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:34.450
Supreme Court after Sandra Day O'Connor's
retirement in 2006.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:42.299
Our Biji was the only woman on the Supreme
Court, a cultural icon well into her 80s,

00:01:42.299 --> 00:01:46.770
really a rock star in the political, in the
social and the cultural world.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:52.700
There is no way to say all of the things that
could be said about Ruth Bader Ginsburg.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:02:01.079
Now, there is a criticism that she should
have retired when she was, say, 82 years old

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:07.299
already with some health problems to ensure
that Barack Obama would have gotten a clean

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:13.069
chance to choose her replacement to preempt
the situation we now see.

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:15.099
And that is a fair criticism.

00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:19.939
And it's in the past and there's nothing we
can do about it now.

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:21.400
And so what is the story now?

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:30.659
Well, the story now is there are 45 ish days
remaining, maybe 45 exactly until an election

00:02:30.659 --> 00:02:33.060
in which Donald Trump might lose.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:37.840
And Donald Trump and Republicans, most Republicans
have shown that they are committed to filling

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.319
this seat and filling it as soon as possible.

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:43.459
We're going to talk about who might opt out
of that.

00:02:43.459 --> 00:02:48.689
But it is, of course, a huge double standard
in 2016 when Barack Obama was in his last

00:02:48.689 --> 00:02:50.069
year.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:54.690
Republicans said let the people have a say,
let the people vote before we fill the seat.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.219
And indeed, they won on that.

00:02:56.219 --> 00:02:57.310
They held the seat.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.489
They refused to give Barack Obama's nominee,
Merrick Garland, confirmation hearings.

00:03:01.489 --> 00:03:07.069
And ultimately, Donald Trump filled that seat
a year later with Neil Gorsuch to replace

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:08.440
Antonin Scalia.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:12.599
And now seemingly the same situation.

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:17.810
Republicans are saying we have a constitutional
duty to fill the seat without delay and they

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:18.939
are going to try.

00:03:18.939 --> 00:03:24.319
Now, Mitch McConnell's explanation is, well,
it's not really the same situation because

00:03:24.319 --> 00:03:29.689
back in 2016, it was opposite parties in the
Senate and White House.

00:03:29.689 --> 00:03:34.160
It was Barack Obama, a Democrat and Republicans
controlling the Senate.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:41.380
It's different now because it's Republicans
controlling the Senate with a Republican president.

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:42.700
That is their argument.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:44.150
It's a ridiculous argument.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:47.480
It is a double standard, but it doesn't matter.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.629
What matters is, are they going to be able
to do it?

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:53.019
And we're going to talk about that in a moment.

00:03:53.019 --> 00:03:59.340
The legacy of Ruth Bader Ginsburg is one that
will be studied hopefully for a very long

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:00.340
time.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:08.230
It's an inspirational story, not just to young
women about someone who really pushed the

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:17.239
boundaries of what society considered to be
the appropriate or valid roles of women in

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:18.930
society.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:23.050
Really an extraordinary woman, an extraordinary
career.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:30.319
And again, a cultural and legal icon well
into her 80s.

00:04:30.319 --> 00:04:33.190
We will miss her, her legacy.

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:37.660
And now the question is, what will become
of that seat?

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.180
And that's what we're going to talk about
next.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:40.710
We'll talk about process.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:05:57.250
And we'll talk about political implications.

